/// ============ THE REACHABILITY GATE ON A METHOD SIGNATURE ==============
///
/// Spec §3.3 and §6.1. This module imports the trait but NOT the module that
/// declares `Parcel`, so the parameter annotation below is unreachable and
/// must be `E0240`.
///
/// It is pinned separately from `namespace_gate` because the two are reported
/// by DIFFERENT PASSES. A free function's signature is judged in the
/// FunctionSignature pass, which aborts the build before the TypeResolution
/// pass that judges impl-block and inline methods ever runs. Put both in one
/// project and the method arms are never reached -- the project would pass
/// while testing nothing.
///
/// WHAT MAKES THIS WORTH A PROJECT: a failed parameter annotation is not
/// poisoned, it is left unset, and every consumer downstream reads an unset
/// param as `void`. Before the gate reached method signatures this file
/// compiled with EXIT 0 and a `void` parameter where the trait declares
/// `Parcel*` -- the impl silently disagreed with the trait it implements, and
/// the method was dead-code eliminated before anything compared them.
///
/// IF THIS PROJECT STARTS BUILDING: the gate stopped judging method
/// signatures. Do not relax `test.json` -- an exit 0 here is the defect.
///
/// =======================================================================
namespace NamespaceGateMethods::Orphan;

import NamespaceGateMethods::Handler;

type struct Box {
    seen: i32;
}

/// The parameter is deliberately UNUSED. A used one fails downstream anyway
/// (`no method named 'get' found on type 'void'`), which names the symptom;
/// an unused one is the silent case, and it is the one that has to error.
implement trait Handler for struct Box {
    handle(mut &this, p: Parcel*) -> i32 { return this.seen; }
}
